Riemann sum approximation of the total number of liters sold over the $6$-hour period (with three subintervals defined by the given points) is $28$ liters, then what is the value of $k?$ \n\n$\\,\\,k=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534987 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "247e80ec-6b94-56ac-94f4-78dcaa13b56f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534987 #edb/ref 1074 "The area $A$ under the curve $y=r(t)$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{r({t}_{0})+r({t}_{1})+…+r({t}_{n-1})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(r(0)+r(2)+r(4))\\cdot Δt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $r(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $r(t)$ | $1$ | $k$ | $5$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following approximation to the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+k+5)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(k+6)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2k+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total number of liters sold by Maya. Since we are given that the left Riemann sum approximation of the total number of liters sold is $28,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+12 & =28 \\\\ 2k & =16 \\\\ k & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534991 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534991 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534991 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534991 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346698" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534991 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534991 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the area under the curve $y=2{x}^{2}-1$ over the interval $[1,5]$ using a left Riemann sum with step size $Δx=1.$\n\nThe estimated area under the curve $y=2{x}^{2}-1$ over the interval $[1,5]$ is {{field-1}}.\nSince the function is strictly {{field-2}}\nin the interval,  the approximation is an\n{{field-3}}\nof the actual area." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534991 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2a6ffc0a-296f-5d33-ab74-d79dec06ff68" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186534991 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/67/671d5efc8abc559cf638bfbfdda76bddb0b8d5c2d9134dffbbf7eab0e2f0b25a.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $7$ | $17$ | $31$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+7+17+31)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =56\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =56\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is strictly $\\text{increasing}$ over the interval $[1,5],$ the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an $\\text{underestimate}$ of the actual area." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535007 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535007 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86288" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535007 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535007 #edb/ref 1071 "The table below gives the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[1,10]$ with a regular step size. Estimate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[1,10]$ using a left Riemann sum with $3$ equal subintervals.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $4$ | $7$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $2$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535007 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4bbaa29d-7536-57fa-abdb-fc58099b0936" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535007 #edb/ref 1074 "The area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f({x}_{0})+f({x}_{1})+…+f({x}_{n-1})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(f(1)+f(4)+f(7))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the interval $[1,10]$ is divided into $3$ equal subintervals, the width of each subinterval is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{10-1}{3}=3.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at the left endpoints:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $4$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+2+0)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535010 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535010 #edb/ref 1069 "q-364487" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535010 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535010 #edb/ref 1071 "Estimate the area $A$ under the curve $y=4{x}^{2}-1$ over the interval $[1,3]$ using a left Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535010 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "52c6a4cd-b903-5d26-bd7b-6413b0fd0ae1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535010 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/27/275b62fbec1c1a550cb23b481ec54c1e12d3d42e70cf95cd917f1b932638add4.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f({x}_{0})+f({x}_{1})+…+f({x}_{n-1})}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(f(1)+f(1.5)+f(2)+f(2.5))\\cdot 0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)=4{x}^{2}-1$ at each left endpoint:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $2.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $8$ | $15$ | $24$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3+8+15+24)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =50\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535022 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535022 