1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n\nUse a left Riemann sum for $w(t)$ over the interval $[0,7]$ with three subintervals to approximate Daniel's total weight loss." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535027 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7303a5f4-0c27-5036-a2ee-f35a42950785" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535027 #edb/ref 1074 "In this case, we have $n=3$ rectangles. Using the left Riemann sum, the area is approximated as\n\n$$\nA≈w(0)Δ{t}_{1}+w(1)Δ{t}_{2}+w(3)Δ{t}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{t}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $t$-value and the next one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{t}_{i}={t}_{i+1}-{t}_{i}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δt$'s.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $1$ | $3$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δt$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $-$ |\n| $w(t)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =1+4+4 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total weight lost by Daniel. He lost approximately $9$ pounds." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535029 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535029 #edb/ref 1069 "q-51130" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535029 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535029 #edb/ref 1071 "The following table shows the speed $v$, in $\\text{km/min},$ at certain times during a family car trip over a period of $60$ minutes.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{min})$ | $60$ | $70$ | $80$ | $95$ | $110$ | $120$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v\\,(\\text{km/min})$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1.5$ |\n\nAssuming that the family traveled $130\\,\\text{km}$ during the first hour of the trip, use the left Riemann sum to approximate the total distance traveled during the first two hours.\n\n*Hint: The distance traveled is given by the area under the speed-time graph.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535029 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "79154f2c-c86a-5c07-9c5a-51aae4f7090e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535029 #edb/ref 1074 "In this case, we have $n=5$ rectangles. Using the left Riemann sum, the area is approximated as\n\n$$\nA≈v(60)Δ{t}_{1}+v(70)Δ{t}_{2}+v(80)Δ{t}_{3}+v(95)Δ{t}_{4}+v(110)Δ{t}_{5},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{t}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $t$-value and the next one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{t}_{i}={t}_{i+1}-{t}_{i}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δt$'s.\n\n| $t$ | $60$ | $70$ | $80$ | $95$ | $110$ | $120$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δt$ | $10$ | $10$ | $15$ | $15$ | $10$ | $-$ |\n| $v(t)$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1.5$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈10\\cdot 1.5+10\\cdot 2+15\\cdot 1+15\\cdot 1+10\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =15+20+15+15+20 \\\\  & =85\\,\\text{km}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the given context, the area under the curve represents the distance covered in $60$ minutes of travel, from $t=60$ minutes to $t=120$ minutes. The distance traveled in the first two hours is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d=130+85=215\\,\\text{km}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535034 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535034 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534932 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535034 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535034 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346697" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535034 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535034 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the area under the curve $y=4x+1$ over the interval $[0,1]$ using a left Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.25.$\n\nThe estimated area under the curve $y=4x+1$ over the interval $[0,1]$ is {{field-1}}.\nSince the function is strictly {{field-2}}\nin the interval,  the approximation is an\n{{field-3}}\nof the actual area." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535034 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7e9c9afd-571f-54f0-b5de-d6a5e5729d5e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535034 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/e8/e8cd8966d69260c77685d349c97eceb1545686d347f6cdb827ad470880797bf5.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0)+f(0.25)+f(0.5)+f(0.75)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ | $0.75$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+2+3+4)\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =10\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is strictly $\\text{increasing}$ over the interval $[0,1],$ the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an $\\text{underestimate}$ of the actual area." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535036 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535036 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86298" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535036 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535036 #edb/ref 1071 "The following table shows the rate of change of a city's temperature over a $10$-hour period, starting at $2\\,\\text{a.m}.$\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{h})$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $T\\,{(}^{∘}\\text{C}/\\text{h})$ | $1$ | $k$ | $4$ | $2$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n\nIf the left Riemann sum approximation of the total change in the temperature from $2\\,\\text{a.m}$ to $12\\,\\text{p.m}$ has a value of $26{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C},$ what is the value of $k?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535036 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "84844930-f95f-5f3a-8037-4adc201a50fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535036 #edb/ref 1074 "The area $A$ under the curve $y=T(t)$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{T({t}_{0})+T({t}_{1})+…+T({t}_{n-1})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(T(2)+T(4)+T(6)+T(8)+T(10))\\cdot Δt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $T(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $T(t)$ | $1$ | $k$ | $4$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following approximation to the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+k+4+2+3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2(k+10) \\\\  & =2k+20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total change in the temperature from $2\\,\\text{a.m}$ to $12\\,\\text{p.m}.$ Since we are given that the left Riemann sum approximation of the total change in the temperature is $26{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+20 & =26 \\\\ 2k & =6 \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535040 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535040 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74503" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535040 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535040 #edb/ref 1071 "Estimate the area under the curve $y={x}^{2}-2x+2$ over the interval $[1,3]$ using a left Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$ Is this an overestimate or an underestimate?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535040 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8bc09582-a4d5-5866-bf69-244d81d8d845" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535040 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/2c/2cd664908ea218fecf5a33ea3312bd883f5495d64fc0da8387632a7d2e17c26a.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(1)+f(1.5)+f(2)+f(2.5)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $2.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $1.25$ | $2$ | $3.25$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+1.25+2+3.25)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =7.5\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =3.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *strictly increasing* over the interval $[1,3],$ the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an *underestimate* of the actual area." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535045 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535045 #edb/ref 1069 "q-315714" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535045 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535045 #edb/ref 1071 "The following table shows some values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[0,8].$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $5$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $4$ | $6$ | $5$ | $9$ |\n\nUse the left Riemann sum, with the four subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area $A$ under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[0,8].$ \n\n$A≈$ \n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535045 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "991c1ace-0aaf-57b9-84aa-e45b1d07c8fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535045 #edb/ref 1074 "In this case, we have $n=4$ rectangles. Using the left Riemann sum, the area is approximated as\n\n$$\nA≈f(0)Δ{x}_{1}+f(2)Δ{x}_{2}+f(5)Δ{x}_{3}+f(6)Δ{x}_{4},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{x}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the next one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{x}_{i}={x}_{i+1}-{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δx$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $5$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δx$ | $2$ | $3$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $4$ | $6$ | $5$ | $9$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈3\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 3+6\\cdot 1+5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =6+12+6+10 \\\\  & =34.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535046 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535046 #edb/ref 1069 "q-315694" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535046 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535046 #edb/ref 1071 "The table below gives the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[2,8]$ with a regular step size.\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-3$ | $5$ | $k$ | $1$ |\n\nIf the left Riemann sum approximation of the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ with $3$ subintervals defined by the given points has a value of $18$, then what must be the value of $k?$ \n\n$\\,\\,k=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535046 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9be6577c-8d1d-5823-8ae8-eea53999e09f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535046 #edb/ref 1074 "The area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f({x}_{0})+f({x}_{1})+f({x}_{2})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(f(2)+f(4)+f(6))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the points are $2,4,6,8$ with three subintervals, the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{8-2}{3}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each left-endpoint:\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-3$ | $5$ | $k$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following approximation to the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈((-3)+5+k)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(2+k)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are given that $A=18$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4+2k & =18 \\\\ 2k & =14 \\\\ k & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535059 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535059 #edb/ref 1069 "q-74490" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535059 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535059 #edb/ref 1071 "Estimate the area under the curve $y=3x-{x}^{2}$ over the interval $[0.5,2.5]$ using a left Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535059 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b62ef58e-043d-550b-83fb-b95a07d27915" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535059 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/0e/0e3f499cbc36c9dca7e759b67a8fd9e94959039f3403832e1d60b9ed598c44dd.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f({x}_{0})+f({x}_{1})+…+f({x}_{n-1})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(f(0.5)+f(1)+f(1.5)+f(2))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1.25$ | $2$ | $2.25$ | $2$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1.25+2+2.25+2)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =7.5\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =3.75\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535060 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535060 #edb/ref 1069 "q-115695" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535060 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535060 #edb/ref 1071 "Estimate the area under the curve $y=-{x}^{2}+3x+1$ over the interval $[1.5,3.5]$ using a left Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$ Is this an overestimate or an underestimate?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535060 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b8274948-1d4b-57b0-9024-c09c00401038" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535060 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/ad/ad1e85721eb2142f5cad422530aeed86c0919f34261d19e11e9f056cf355b387.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(1.5)+f(2)+f(2.5)+f(3)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $2.5$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3.25$ | $3$ | $2.25$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3.25+3+2.25+1)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =9.5\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =4.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *strictly decreasing* over the interval $[1.5,3.5],$ the rectangles reach *above* the function, which means our approximation is an *overestimate* of the actual area." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535071 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535071 #edb/ref 1069 "q-115676" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535071 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535071 #edb/ref 1071 "A tank is filling with soda. The table below gives the rate $R(t)$, in liters per minute, at which the tank is filling at certain moments since it started.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{minutes})$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)\\,(\\text{liters/minute})$ | $30$ | $40$ | $60$ | $70$ | $90$ |\n\nGiven that the tank is empty when $t=0,$ use a left Riemann sum for $R(t)$ over the interval $[0,12]$ with four subintervals to approximate the total volume of soda in the tank after $12$ minutes." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535071 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d4c5ef62-20c9-5f14-af52-91dedd4c1fc7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535071 #edb/ref 1074 "First, let's compute the step size $Δt,$ which is just the width of each rectangle. In this case, the endpoints of our interval are $a=0$ and $b=12,$ and there are $n=4$ rectangles, so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{12-0}{4}=3.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{R({t}_{0})+R({t}_{1})+…+R({t}_{n-1})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(R(0)+R(3)+R(6)+R(9))\\cdot Δt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $R(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)$ | $30$ | $40$ | $60$ | $70$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(30+40+60+70)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =200\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =600\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total volume in the tank. So, the volume is $600$ liters." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535080 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534882 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535080 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71866" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535080 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535080 #edb/ref 1071 "The following table shows some values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[0,10].$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $4$ | $5$ | $9$ | $15$ |\n\nUse the left Riemann sum, with the four subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[0,10].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535080 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e9dcd5b8-f37b-55de-ab7a-6d8ec569a890" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535080 #edb/ref 1074 "In this case, we have $n=4$ rectangles. Using the left Riemann sum, the area is approximated as\n\n$$\nA≈f(0)Δ{x}_{1}+f(3)Δ{x}_{2}+f(5)Δ{x}_{3}+f(7)Δ{x}_{4},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{x}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the next one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{x}_{i}={x}_{i+1}-{x}_{i}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δx$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δx$ | $3$ | $2$ | $2$ | $3$ | $-$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $4$ | $5$ | $9$ | $15$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈1\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 2+5\\cdot 2+9\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3+8+10+27 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535083 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534952 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535083 #edb/ref 1069 "q-115688" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535083 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535083 #edb/ref 1071 "The table below gives the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[-3,3]$ with a regular step size. If the left Riemann sum approximation of the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ with $3$ subintervals defined by the given points has a value of $4$, then what must be the value of $k?$\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $k$ | $4$ | $5$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535083 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f3cd5193-df6c-5672-95df-52f56264b423" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535083 #edb/ref 1074 "The area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f({x}_{0})+f({x}_{1})+…+f({x}_{n-1})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(f(-3)+f(-1)+f(1))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $k$ | $4$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following approximation to the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+k+4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(5+k)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =10+2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are given that $A$ must be $4$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10+2k & =4 \\\\ 2k & =-6 \\\\ k & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535090 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186534890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535090 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86296" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535090 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535090 #edb/ref 1071 "John is selling juice at a market stall. The table below gives the rate $r(t)$ of juice sold over a period of $6$ hours, from $1\\,\\text{p.m.}$ to $7\\,\\text{p.m.}$\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{hours})$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $r(t)\\,(\\text{liters/hour})$ | $k$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\nIf the left Riemann sum approximation of the total number of liters John sold during the given period has a value of $16$ liters, what is the value of $k?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535090 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fd327109-8d69-58f0-a86a-7b3b70080707" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186535090 #edb/ref 1074 "The area $A$ under the curve $y=r(t)$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{r({t}_{0})+r({t}_{1})+…+r({t}_{n-1})}})\\cdot \\,\\,{{Δt}_{⏟}}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(r(1)+r(3)+r(5))\\cdot Δt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $r(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $r(t)$ | $k$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following approximation to the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(k+2+3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2(k+5) \\\\  & =2k+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the given context, the area under the curve represents the total number of liters sold by John. Since we are given that the left Riemann sum approximation of the total number of liters sold is $16,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+10 & =16 \\\\ 2k & =6 \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
